1
00:00:00,484 --> 00:00:04,919
This is what happens if Earth stops spinning. For one second.

2
00:00:06,696 --> 00:00:09,572
And trust me, this is the nice part of town.

3
00:00:09,660 --> 00:00:12,141
Let's rewind ten seconds.

4
00:00:12,981 --> 00:00:15,950
Right now, you are moving. Fast.

5
00:00:16,375 --> 00:00:17,803
Earth spins,

6
00:00:18,464 --> 00:00:24,666
and at the equator the ground carries you east at about 1,670 kilometres an hour.

7
00:00:25,244 --> 00:00:27,803
That's faster than the speed of sound.

8
00:00:27,997 --> 00:00:35,420
Further north it's slower: about 1,180 at 45 degrees. At the poles, almost nothing.

9
00:00:35,446 --> 00:00:37,435
You just turn on the spot.

10
00:00:38,297 --> 00:00:43,434
So: Tuesday, 7:59 and fifty seconds. Coffee in hand.

11
00:00:43,719 --> 00:00:45,397
SAM: Is that clock fast?

12
00:00:45,591 --> 00:00:49,235
At eight o'clock exactly, the planet slams on the brakes.

13
00:00:49,606 --> 00:00:52,557
The ground stops. You don't.

14
00:00:54,919 --> 00:01:02,031
Everything bolted to the bedrock stops with it. Buildings. Bridges. Mountains.

15
00:01:02,503 --> 00:01:10,920
Everything that isn't keeps going east at full speed. People. Cars. Oceans. The air.

16
00:01:11,760 --> 00:01:20,309
How hard is that stop? Going from 465 metres a second to zero in one second is about 47 g.

17
00:01:20,772 --> 00:01:26,715
Fighter pilots train to survive nine. Most of us black out long before that.

18
00:01:26,960 --> 00:01:29,850
Whatever is bolted down feels all of it.

19
00:01:30,712 --> 00:01:35,201
Then the air arrives. The atmosphere doesn't stop either.

20
00:01:35,717 --> 00:01:40,986
At the equator that's a wind of around 1,670 kilometres an hour.

21
00:01:41,280 --> 00:01:45,762
The fastest tornado wind ever measured was about 480.

22
00:01:46,446 --> 00:01:51,201
The oceans keep going too, piling up against every coast that faces east.

23
00:01:51,358 --> 00:01:57,360
How big are those waves? Nobody can say for sure. Let's just say: big.

24
00:01:58,524 --> 00:02:01,257
And here's the part nobody mentions.

25
00:02:01,717 --> 00:02:07,372
One second later, Earth starts spinning again. A second lurch, the other way.

26
00:02:07,826 --> 00:02:10,985
One second. Two disasters.

27
00:02:12,103 --> 00:02:16,750
So who survives? Someone standing exactly on a pole.

28
00:02:16,847 --> 00:02:22,819
Meet Kai, at the South Pole. Kai was barely moving to begin with, so Kai just... wobbles.

29
00:02:23,101 --> 00:02:25,629
KAI: Huh. Anyone else spill their tea?

30
00:02:25,649 --> 00:02:28,229
And no, there are no penguins to tell.

31
00:02:28,264 --> 00:02:31,887
They live on the coast, about 1,400 kilometres away.

32
00:02:32,927 --> 00:02:37,212
Good news: nothing in the universe is going to stop Earth in one second.

33
00:02:37,639 --> 00:02:41,815
Bad news: something is slowing it down. Right now.

34
00:02:42,118 --> 00:02:43,149
The Moon.

35
00:02:43,169 --> 00:02:45,217
Its tides act like a brake,

36
00:02:45,237 --> 00:02:48,978
and every century the day gets a couple of milliseconds longer.

37
00:02:49,396 --> 00:02:53,789
Wait about 200 million years, and you get a 25-hour day.

38
00:02:54,221 --> 00:02:56,857
Finally, time to finish that coffee.

39
00:02:57,897 --> 00:03:03,181
So. You get ten seconds' warning. Where on Earth do you run?

40
00:03:03,249 --> 00:03:07,218
Tell me your city in the comments, and I'll tell you how fast you were going.

41
00:03:07,541 --> 00:03:10,342
Next time: what if the Moon vanished tonight?
